Package: nspawnmgr
Version: 0.4.0
Section: web
Priority: optional
Architecture: all
Depends: openssh-server, openssl, dnsmasq, systemd-container
Maintainer: nspawnmgr packaging <packaging@nspawnmgr.invalid>
Description: Web manager for systemd-nspawn containers with Apache Guacamole access
 nspawnmgr provides a web UI for creating, starting, stopping, and accessing
 systemd-nspawn containers via Guacamole (SSH/RDP). Deploys as nspawnmgr.war
 and auth.war into its own bundled Tomcat 9 instance, alongside guacamole.war.
 .
 IMPORTANT: tomcat9 is NOT an apt dependency of this package either -
 previously it was, but the apt tomcat9 package's own availability varies
 enough by Debian/Ubuntu/Mint release that this package now bundles the same
 vanilla upstream Apache Tomcat binary distribution instead (a current
 patch release, not whatever an apt archive happens to carry) and owns the
 whole instance itself: /opt/tomcat9, its own tomcat system user, and its
 own tomcat9.service systemd unit. If a previous version of this package
 (which did depend on apt tomcat9) is already installed, remove that
 package's tomcat9 first to avoid two instances both trying to bind the
 same port.
 .
 IMPORTANT: guacd and guacamole-tomcat are NOT apt dependencies of this
 package, deliberately - as of this package's build, neither is packaged for
 any current Debian/Ubuntu/Mint release at all (confirmed by searching the
 actual archives, not just "varies by release"). guacd is bundled instead: a
 self-contained build (own OpenSSL 3.x, minimal FFmpeg, FreeRDP2, libssh2 -
 see /usr/share/doc/nspawnmgr/guacd-bundle-README.md) that postinst extracts
 to /opt/guacd-bundle and runs as its own systemd service (guacd.service),
 no system guacd package needed. guacamole.war itself is also bundled
 directly (the official Apache release, not built by this project) and
 deployed into the same Tomcat instance as nspawnmgr.war/auth.war - unlike
 guacd, there was never a licensing/rebuild concern here, just no apt
 package to depend on. GUACAMOLE_HOME (/etc/guacamole) is created too, with
 a minimal guacamole.properties pointed at the bundled guacd instance - see
 /usr/share/doc/nspawnmgr/administrator-guide.md section 7 for what's still
 manual (the database-backed auth extension, since that needs real
 credentials this package can't know).
 .
 IMPORTANT: this package's Guacamole integration also requires the
 guacamole-auth-jdbc extension, which is never an apt package on any release
 and is not pulled in by this Depends: line. It is not optional: nspawnmgr
 manages Guacamole connections/users through Guacamole's REST API, which only
 exists when Guacamole is running a database-backed auth extension - the
 stock user-mapping.xml auth Guacamole ships with has no such API. Like guacd,
 it's bundled directly rather than downloaded at install time - postinst
 extracts this package's own vendored copy (no network needed) and also
 copies both MySQL/PostgreSQL JDBC driver jars into GUACAMOLE_HOME/lib (this
 package already bundles both, for nspawnmgr's own unrelated database use).
 Copying the extension JAR itself into GUACAMOLE_HOME/extensions, running its
 schema, and creating the Guacamole admin account are still manual steps -
 see /usr/share/doc/nspawnmgr/administrator-guide.md section 7.
 .
 This package assumes nspawnmgr manages containers on the same host it runs
 on. Managing a remote nspawn host is a manually-configured, documented-only
 scenario — see /usr/share/doc/nspawnmgr/administrator-guide.md.
 .
 apache2-utils is a real apt dependency (unlike everything bundled above) -
 it provides /usr/bin/rotatelogs, which tomcat9.service pipes Tomcat's own
 stdout/stderr through to produce daily-rotated catalina.out.<date> files in
 /opt/tomcat9/logs, viewable from nspawnmgr's own "View log" page.
 .
 dnsmasq is also a real apt dependency, unlike guacd/Tomcat above - it lets
 managed containers resolve each other by their nspawnmgr name. postinst
 drops a config scoping it to container veths only (never reachable from the
 host's own LAN/uplink interface) and pointing it at a hosts file nspawnmgr
 keeps in sync with each running container's current internal address. See
 /usr/share/doc/nspawnmgr/administrator-guide.md's Container networking
 section for the one remaining manual step (a systemd-networkd drop-in that
 hands out this resolver's address via DHCP - not something this package can
 safely edit, since it doesn't own that file either).
